```text
nodes  on a tie  rounds  first round that breaks a rule
    3  approve       16  follower 1 lies (honest verdict DECLINE): validity, honest followers decide {2=APPROVE}
    3  decline       16  follower 1 lies (honest verdict APPROVE): validity, honest followers decide {2=DECLINE}
    3  leader        16  the leader lies (honest verdict APPROVE): agreement, honest followers decide {1=APPROVE, 2=DECLINE}
    4  approve       40  none
    4  decline       40  none
    4  leader        40  none
    5  approve       96  none
    5  decline       96  none
    5  leader        96  the leader lies (honest verdict APPROVE): agreement, honest followers decide {1=APPROVE, 2=APPROVE, 3=DECLINE, 4=DECLINE}
```

In the output, validity means an honest leader's verdict wins, and agreement means every honest follower decides the same. With 3 nodes every tie rule fails. A fixed rule lets a lying follower overrule an honest leader (validity), and trusting the leader lets a lying leader split the followers (agreement). With 4 nodes all 40 rounds pass under every rule, because each honest follower hears three verdicts and the liar spoils only one. With 5 nodes a lying leader can send 2 APPROVE and 2 DECLINE. Every honest follower then hears the same tie, and a fixed rule turns it into the same decision everywhere. But "trust the leader" makes each follower decide by its own message from the leader, so they split 2 and 2.

